      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "308ed18d-3d12-59f1-84f9-d4c570cd5721",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Roxie Hart, Chicago, Chicago Gal\n- People: William A. Wellman, Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley, Spring Byington, Beulah Annan\n- Topics: Chicago adaptations, Phil Silvers\n\nFacts:\n- Roxie Hart, also known as Chicago or Chicago Gal, is a 1942 American comedy film directed by William A. Wellman.\n- Roxie Hart stars Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley, and Spring Byington.\n- The story inspired by Beulah Annan was adapted into a 1942 romantic comedy film titled Roxie Hart, named for the character Annan inspired.\n- Phil Silvers was born on May 11, 1911, and was an American entertainer and comedic actor.\n- Phil Silvers was known as \"The King of Chutzpah\" and died on November 1, 1985.\n- The play connected to Beulah Annan was also adapted into a 1927 silent film, a 1975 stage musical, and a 2002 movie musical titled Chicago.\n- The 2002 movie musical Chicago won the Academy Award for Best Picture.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f547ace9-6674-5810-b0d9-f0da65756c7a",
        "text": "Page: Roxie Hart (film). Sentence: Roxie Hart, also known as Chicago or Chicago Gal, is a 1942 American comedy film directed by William A. Wellman and starring Ginger Rogers, Adolphe Menjou, George Montgomery, Nigel Bruce, Phil Silvers, William Frawley and Spring Byington.\nPage: Phil Silvers. Sentence: Phil Silvers (May 11, 1911 – November 1, 1985) was an American entertainer and comedic actor, known as \"The King of Chutzpah\".\nPage: Beulah Annan. Sentence: The play has been adapted into a 1927 silent film, 1975 stage musical, and 2002 movie musical (which won the Academy Award for Best Picture), all with that title, and a 1942 romantic comedy film, \"Roxie Hart\", named for the character Annan inspired.",
